Top 50 Core Java Interview Questions with Answers
These are commonly asked Core Java interview questions for freshers and entry-level Java developers, especially in campus placements and technical interviews.

I've arranged them topic-wise, starting with fundamentals and moving towards advanced concepts.
1. Java Fundamentals
1. What is Java?

Java is a high-level, object-oriented, platform-independent programming language.

Key features:
- Object-oriented
- Platform-independent
- Secure
- Robust
- Multithreaded
- Automatic garbage collection
- Portable
2. Why is Java platform-independent?

Java is platform-independent because Java source code is compiled into bytecode.
- .java → Java source code
- .class → Bytecode
- JVM executes bytecode on different operating systems.
This follows the principle: Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA).

3. What is the difference between JDK, JRE and JVM?
JDK	JRE	JVM
Java Development Kit	Java Runtime Environment	Java Virtual Machine
Used for development	Used to run Java applications	Executes bytecode
Includes development tools	Provides runtime libraries and JVM	Executes .class files
Contains JRE components and tools	Contains JVM and libraries	Part of JRE

4. What are the main features of Java?
1. Object-oriented
2. Platform-independent
3. Simple
4. Secure
5. Robust
6. Multithreaded
7. Portable
8. Distributed
9. Dynamic
5. Is Java a pure object-oriented language?

No. Java is not purely object-oriented because it supports primitive data types such as int, char, float, and boolean, which are not objects.

6. What is bytecode in Java?

Bytecode is the intermediate code generated by the Java compiler.

The JVM converts bytecode into machine-specific instructions for execution.

7. What is the difference between compiler and interpreter?
Compiler	Interpreter
Translates source code into bytecode	JVM executes bytecode
Compilation happens before execution	Execution happens at runtime
Reports compilation errors	Can encounter runtime errors

Java uses both compilation and runtime interpretation/JIT compilation.

8. What is the main method in Java?

The main method is the entry point of a traditional standalone Java application.
public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println("Hello Java");
}


- public: Accessible to JVM.
- static: No object required.
- void: Returns nothing.
- main: Method name.
- String[] args: Command-line arguments.
2. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)
9. What are the four pillars of OOP?
1. Encapsulation: Wrapping data and methods into a single unit.
2. Inheritance: Acquiring properties of another class.
3. Polymorphism: One interface, multiple implementations.
4. Abstraction: Hiding implementation details and showing essential functionality.
10. What is a class and an object?
- Class: Blueprint for creating objects.
- Object: Instance of a class.
class Student {
    int id;
    String name;
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();
        s.id = 101;
        s.name = "Ganesh";
    }
}


11. What is inheritance?

Inheritance allows one class to acquire the properties and methods of another class using extends.
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}


12. What are the types of inheritance in Java?
- Single inheritance
- Multilevel inheritance
- Hierarchical inheritance
Java does not support multiple inheritance through classes, but it supports multiple inheritance of type through interfaces.

13. What is polymorphism?

Polymorphism means one name having multiple forms.

Two types:
- Compile-time polymorphism – Method overloading.
- Runtime polymorphism – Method overriding.
14. What is method overloading?

Having multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists in the same class.
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
}


15. What is method overriding?

When a child class provides its own implementation of a method already defined in its parent class.
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}


16. Difference between overloading and overriding?
Overloading	Overriding
Same method name	Same method signature
Different parameters	Same parameters
Usually within same class	Requires inheritance
Compile-time polymorphism	Runtime polymorphism
Return type alone cannot differentiate methods	Return type must be same or covariant

17. What is abstraction?

Abstraction hides implementation details and displays only necessary functionality.

It can be achieved using:
- Abstract classes
- Interfaces
18. What is encapsulation?

Encapsulation means wrapping variables and methods together and restricting direct access to data.
class Student {
    private int age;

    public void setAge(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}


19. Difference between abstract class and interface?
Abstract class	Interface
Uses abstract class	Uses interface
Can have constructors	Cannot have constructors
Can have instance variables	Fields are implicitly public static final
Supports abstract and concrete methods	Supports abstract, default, static and private methods
A class can extend only one class	A class can implement multiple interfaces

20. What is the difference between this and super?
- this refers to the current object.
- super refers to the immediate parent class.
class Parent {
    int x = 10;
}

class Child extends Parent {
    int x = 20;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(this.x);  // 20
        System.out.println(super.x); // 10
    }
}


3. Strings in Java
21. Why is String immutable in Java?

String is immutable because once a String object is created, its value cannot be changed.

Benefits:
- Security
- Thread safety
- String pool optimization
- Reliable hash code caching
22. Difference between String, StringBuilder and StringBuffer?
String	StringBuilder	StringBuffer
Immutable	Mutable	Mutable
Cannot modify original object	Can modify original object	Can modify original object
Thread-safe due to immutability	Not synchronized	Synchronized
Suitable for fixed text	Fast string modifications	Thread-safe modifications

23. Difference between == and .equals()?
- == compares primitive values or object references.
- .equals() compares object contents when properly overridden.
String s1 = new String("Java");
String s2 = new String("Java");

System.out.println(s1 == s2);      // false
System.out.println(s1.equals(s2)); // true


24. What is the String Constant Pool?

It is a special area of heap memory where Java maintains pooled String objects to reuse identical string literals.
String a = "Java";
String b = "Java";

System.out.println(a == b); // true


25. What is the difference between concat() and +?

Both can concatenate strings.
String a = "Hello";
String b = "Java";

System.out.println(a + b);
System.out.println(a.concat(b));


Both produce HelloJava.
4. Exception Handling
26. What is an exception in Java?

An exception is an unwanted event that interrupts the normal execution of a program.

Examples:
- ArithmeticException
- NullPointerException
- ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
- IOException
27. Difference between checked and unchecked exceptions?
Checked Exception	Unchecked Exception
Checked at compile time	Not checked at compile time
Must handle or declare	Handling is not mandatory
IOException	NullPointerException
SQLException	ArithmeticException

28. Difference between throw and throws?
- throw: Used to explicitly throw an exception.
- throws: Declares possible exceptions in a method signature.
void checkAge(int age) {
    if (age < 18) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Not eligible");
    }
}


void readFile() throws IOException {
    // File reading logic
}


29. What is finally in Java?

The finally block normally executes whether an exception occurs or not.
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Exception occurred");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Finally executed");
}


30. Difference between final, finally and finalize?
Keyword	Meaning
final	Restricts modification, overriding or inheritance depending on usage
finally	Exception-handling block
finalize()	Deprecated garbage-collection-related method

Note: finalize() is deprecated and should not be used for resource cleanup.
5. Collections Framework
31. What is the Java Collections Framework?

It is a set of interfaces and classes used to store and manipulate groups of objects.

Main interfaces:
- List
- Set
- Queue
- Map (part of the framework, but not a subtype of Collection)
32. Difference between ArrayList and LinkedList?
ArrayList	LinkedList
Dynamic array	Doubly linked list
Fast random access	Slow random access
Insertion in the middle can require shifting	Insertion after locating position requires link updates
Less memory overhead	More memory overhead

33. Difference between List, Set and Map?
List	Set	Map
Allows duplicates	Does not allow duplicate elements	Stores key-value pairs
Maintains order depending on implementation	Ordering depends on implementation	Key must be unique
ArrayList	HashSet	HashMap

34. Difference between HashMap and Hashtable?
HashMap	Hashtable
Not synchronized	Synchronized
Allows one null key	Does not allow null keys
Allows null values	Does not allow null values
Generally faster	Generally slower

35. How does HashMap work internally?

HashMap stores data in key-value pairs.

Working:
1. Calls hashCode() on the key.
2. Calculates the bucket index.
3. Stores the key-value pair in that bucket.
4. If a collision occurs, it handles it using a linked list or a balanced tree under suitable conditions.
5. Uses equals() to identify matching keys.
36. Difference between HashSet and TreeSet?
HashSet	TreeSet
Uses hashing	Uses a sorted tree
No guaranteed iteration order	Maintains sorted order
Generally O(1) average operations	O(log n) operations
Allows one null element in typical usage	Does not allow null with natural ordering

37. What is an Iterator?

Iterator is used to traverse elements in a collection.
ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(20);
list.add(30);

Iterator<Integer> it = list.iterator();

while (it.hasNext()) {
    System.out.println(it.next());
}


6. Multithreading
38. What is multithreading?

Multithreading is the process of executing multiple threads concurrently within a single program.

Benefits:
- Better CPU utilization
- Improved responsiveness
- Concurrent task execution
39. What is the difference between process and thread?
Process	Thread
Independent executing program	Smallest unit of execution within a process
Has its own memory space	Shares process memory
More resource-intensive	Less resource-intensive

40. How can you create a thread in Java?

Two common approaches:
1. Extending Thread class.
2. Implementing Runnable interface.
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread running");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
    }
}


41. Difference between start() and run()?
- start() creates a new thread and invokes its run() method.
- run() called directly behaves like a normal method call and does not create a new thread.
42. What is synchronization?

Synchronization controls access to shared resources by multiple threads to prevent race conditions.
synchronized void display() {
    System.out.println("Synchronized method");
}


43. What is deadlock?

Deadlock occurs when two or more threads wait indefinitely for resources held by each other.

Example:
- Thread 1 holds Lock A and waits for Lock B.
- Thread 2 holds Lock B and waits for Lock A.
7. JVM, Memory Management and Advanced Java
44. What is garbage collection?

Garbage collection is the automatic process of identifying and reclaiming memory occupied by objects that are no longer reachable.

45. What is the difference between Stack and Heap memory?
Stack	Heap
Stores method frames and local variables	Stores objects and arrays
Each thread has its own stack	Shared among threads
Automatically managed through method calls	Managed by garbage collector
Generally faster allocation	Generally slower allocation

46. What is a constructor?

A constructor is a special class member used to initialize objects.

Characteristics:
- Same name as the class.
- No return type.
- Called during object creation.
- Can be overloaded.
47. What is the difference between static and non-static?
Static	Non-static
Belongs to class	Belongs to object
Can be accessed using class name	Usually accessed through an object
One class-level copy of static fields	Each object has its own instance fields
Cannot directly access instance members	Can access static and instance members

48. What is a wrapper class?

Wrapper classes convert primitive data types into objects.
Primitive	Wrapper
int	Integer
char	Character
double	Double
float	Float
boolean	Boolean
long	Long

49. What is autoboxing and unboxing?
- Autoboxing: Converting primitive into wrapper object.
- Unboxing: Converting wrapper object into primitive.
int a = 10;

Integer obj = a; // Autoboxing

int b = obj;     // Unboxing


50. What is the difference between Comparable and Comparator?
Comparable	Comparator
Defines natural ordering	Defines custom ordering
Uses compareTo()	Uses compare()
Implemented by the class being sorted	Can be implemented separately
Usually one natural ordering	Multiple custom orderings possible
